== Overview ==

The wealthiest individuals and families in Balboa are primarily founders and controlling shareholders of companies listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]]. The list reflects the structure of the Balboan economy: wealth is concentrated in real estate, retail, telecommunications, commodities, and industrial sectors, with significant representation from family-owned enterprises that were built from the mid-20th century onward and taken public in the privatisation and listing waves of the 1990s and 2000s.

The [[Vidal family]] is the wealthiest family in Balboa, with an estimated net worth of $3.1 billion derived from stakes in three listed companies: [[Finques Superiors]] (30%), a luxury high-rise developer; [[Doble8]] (28%), the largest convenience store chain in southern Abyala, founded by [[Rafael Vidal i Comas]]; and [[Construccions de Sant Cristòfor]] (12%), the largest residential developer. The Vidal family's wealth spans both real estate and retail, reflecting the family's diversification across the Balboan consumer and property markets.

The [[Rahman family]], the second-wealthiest at $2.4 billion, is of Sanrobonian origin and holds an 11% stake in [[Coccola dels distribuïdors Abyala]], the largest Coccola bottler in Abyala. The family are passive shareholders with no management role in the company, which is professionally managed by appointees from [[HAWT Inc.]] The Rahman family's inclusion reflects the cross-border nature of the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor, which lists several companies with significant non-Balboan shareholder bases.

[[Francesca Solà i Verdú]], the only individual on the list who is not listed as part of a family holding, is the founder and Chairman of [[Grup Químic Central]] (GQC) and the wealthiest self-made woman in Balboa. Her $1.2 billion fortune is derived from her 15% stake in GQC, which she founded in 1998. She is also the only person on the list whose wealth is derived from the semiconductor supply chain, and her inclusion reflects the growing importance of high-technology industries to the Balboan economy. She has been labelled a "[[scientist of death]]" ({{lang|ca|científica de la mort}}) for her role in the PFAS pollution scandals in [[Jala]] province.

The [[Rouche family]], founded by [[Henri Rouche]] (1922–1984), a Black Balboan entrepreneur, is the wealthiest family in Balboa with origins in the Black Balboan community. The family's $2.0 billion fortune is derived from its 28% stake in [[HRX]], the largest department store operator in Balboa, which Henri Rouche founded as a single variety store in 1958.

The [[Arimany family]], the third-wealthiest at $2.3 billion, traces its fortune to [[Lluís Arimany i Puig]], who led the consortium that acquired [[Abyala Telecom]] (then the state telecom monopoly) from AC receivership in 1953. The family retains a 12% stake in the company, now the largest telecommunications provider in southern Abyala, and [[Jordi Arimany i Soler]], Lluís's grandson, serves as Chairman.

== Methodology ==

Net worth figures are calculated by multiplying the market capitalisation of each listed company by the ownership percentage disclosed in the company's infobox on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor. The following limitations apply:

* The list includes only individuals and families with disclosed ownership stakes in companies listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor. Wealthy individuals whose assets are held in private companies, non-listed holdings, or foreign-listed companies are not included.
* Several Bolsa-listed companies, including [[CIEA]] ($140 billion market cap), [[Grup Ciutat Nova]] ($7.0 billion), [[ECafè]] ($5.0 billion), and [[Mineria de Mario]] ($4.0 billion), do not have individual or family shareholders with disclosed stakes of 5% or more. CIEA, the largest company on the Bolsa, is widely held with no single dominant shareholder.
* The list does not include the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB), the Balboan sovereign wealth fund, or the [[Balboa Pension Fund]] (BPF), both of which hold significant stakes in multiple listed companies but are institutional rather than individual shareholders.
* Net worth figures are based on market capitalisation as of 2026 and do not include private assets, real estate, art collections, or other non-listed holdings. Actual net worth may be higher.
* The [[Solà family]] of [[Recursos del Marañón]] (rank 6) and [[Francesca Solà i Verdú]] (rank 7) are unrelated; Solà is a common Catalan surname.

